id: design.circuit-breaker
kind: design
title: Circuit breaker
aliases: circuit breaker, bulkhead, fail fast
tags: resilience, reliability
questions:
  - how do you stop a failing dependency from taking your service down?
invariant: After a threshold of failures the breaker opens, failing calls immediately without touching the dependency, and half-opens periodically to test recovery.
answer_30s: A circuit breaker trips after repeated failures so calls fail fast instead of piling up on a dead dependency, then probes with limited traffic to detect recovery.
answer_2m: |
  What it protects is not the dependency but your own resources: every waiting
  call holds a thread, a connection and a task, so a hung dependency exhausts the
  pool long before it returns errors. Three states with clear behaviour: closed
  (pass through while failures are below threshold), open (fail immediately for
  a cooldown), half-open (allow a small number of probes). Pair it with
  timeouts and bounded concurrency, because a breaker without a timeout still
  waits; pair it with a degraded response (cached value, partial result,
  queue-for-later) or the fast failure is just a faster outage. The subtlety
  interviewers like: the breaker must be per-dependency and ideally
  per-replica, and the fallback path must not itself depend on the broken thing.
when_to_use:
  - calls to third parties, other services with their own failure modes, anything you cannot fix
avoid_when:
  - in-process calls, or dependencies whose failure is already cheap to absorb
failure_modes:
  - breaker without a timeout, so calls hang before it can trip
  - a fallback that also calls the failed dependency
  - one global breaker for all dependencies, so one slow path blocks unrelated ones
follow_ups:
  - what does the service return while the breaker is open?
related:
  - design.retry-backoff
  - design.timeouts
  - design.load-shedding
  - recipe.resilient-dependent-service
sources:
  - https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/circuit-breaker
